andated by the source. Supplementary benchmarks are optional.", "BF16 is the primary precision; INT4 is a sensitivity check only.", "The pre-registered decision rule thresholds (ρ<0.5 for reduction, CI<0 for negation, d≥0.8 for practical significance, Bonferroni α=0.0083) are proposed and should be confirmed by the researcher before execution.", "LLaMA-2 pre-training data (2.0T tokens) is assumed to be accessible for Tier 2 retraining; if not, proxy checkpoints are the fallback.", "Inference throughput estimates (~50 tok/s for 70B on A100, ~200 tok/s for 13B) are order-of-magnitude estimates from published benchmarks, not measured in this environment."], "uncertainties": ["Whether the execution environment will have GPU access, network for weight downloads, and sufficient disk space (332 GB for Tier 1 weights).", "Whether Meta or the community has published ablated LLaMA-2 checkpoints (reduced pre-training, SFT-only) that could replace retraining in Tier 2.", "The exact token count and data mix of the LLaMA-2 pre-training run (2.0T tokens is the commonly cited figure; the exact composition may affect the 'reduced' variant design).", "Whether the SFT dataset used for LLaMA-2-Chat is fully publicly available (Meta released a subset; the full 117K examples may require license acceptance).", "The appropriate effect-size threshold for 'negation' vs 'reduction' — the proposed ρ<0.5 and d≥0.8 are reasonable defaults but the researcher may prefer different cutoffs.", "Whether the 13B and 70B models were truly trained on identical token budgets (2.0T each) or different ones; if 13B had fewer tokens, the 'Full P' baseline differs by size, confounding the S main effect.", "Quantisation effects on the size gap — INT4 may differentially impact 70B vs 13B, potentially narrowing or widening the size advantage."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-01c8f0d5-328d-4e17-8333-bcd4ebf98dce", "tool_call": {"id": "call-01c8f0d5-328d-4e17-8333-bcd4ebf98dce", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"answer": "# Experiment Design: Does Model Size Necessarily Yield Improved Performance, or Can Insufficient Pre-Training and Unrefined Alignment Negate the Benefit?\n\n**Status: DESIGN ONLY. No experiment has been or will be run in this environment.**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Research Question & Hypotheses\n\n**Primary question (PQ):** Does increasing model size (LLaMA-2-70B vs LLaMA-2-13B) necessarily yield improved performance, or can insufficient pre-training and a lack of refined alignment negate the benefits of a larger parameter count?\n\n**Decomposed into three testable sub-questions:**\n\n| ID | Sub-question | Maps to factor |\n|---|---|---|\n| SQ1 | Holding pre-training and alignment constant, does 70B outperform 13B? | Size (S) main effect |\n| SQ2 | Holding size constant, does alignment refinement (base \u2192 Chat) improve performance, and does the magnitude of that improvement differ by size? | Alignment (A) main effect + S\u00d7A interaction |\n| SQ3 | Holding size constant, does reduced pre-training degrade performance, and does the size advantage shrink or reverse? | Pre-training (P) main effect + S\u00d7P interaction |\n\n**Focal hypothesis (the question's core claim):**\n> H\u2081: There exists a configuration (P*, A*) such that Perf(70B, P*, A*) \u2264 Perf(13B, full-P, full-A), i.e., a larger-but-under-trained-and-poorly-aligned model does not beat a smaller-but-well-trained-and-well-aligned model.\n\n**Null:** H\u2080: For all (P, A), Perf(70B, P, A) > Perf(13B, P, A).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Experimental Design\n\n### 2.1 Factorial Structure\n\nThree factors, each with ordered levels:\n\n| Factor | Symbol | Levels | Operationalisation |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Size** | S | 13 B, 70 B | LLaMA-2 architecture, two published parameter counts |\n| **Pre-training** | P | Full, Reduced (~50 % tokens), Severely Reduced (~25 % tokens) | Token budget relative to the 2.0 T-token LLaMA-2 pre-training run. Data mix held constant (same web/code/academic proportions). |\n| **Alignment** | A | Full (SFT + RLHF), Partial (SFT only), None (base) | Alignment pipeline depth. \"None\" = no instruction tuning, no preference optimisation. |\n\nFull factorial: 2 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3 = **18 cells**. The design is executed in **two tiers** (see \u00a72.2\u20132.3) so that a feasible subset can be run with modest hardware while the full factorial remains available for a well-resourced execution.\n\n### 2.2 Tier 1 \u2014 Feasible Core (Public Checkpoints Only)\n\n**Purpose:** Answer SQ1 and SQ2 with zero retraining. This is the tier a researcher with **2 \u00d7 A100-80 GB** (or 1 \u00d7 H100-80 GB) can execute.\n\n**Models (all publicly released by Meta, HuggingFace `meta-llama/`):**\n\n| Cell ID | Model | S | P | A | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| C1 | `Llama-2-13b-hf` | 13 B | Full | None | Base model |\n| C2 | `Llama-2-13b-chat-hf` | 13 B | Full | Full | SFT + RLHF |\n| C3 | `Llama-2-70b-hf` | 70 B | Full | None | Base model |\n| C4 | `Llama-2-70b-chat-hf` | 70 B | Full | Full | SFT + RLHF |\n\n**What Tier 1 answers:**\n- **SQ1 (size main effect):** C4 vs C2 (70B-Chat vs 13B-Chat) and C3 vs C1 (70B-base vs 13B-base).\n- **SQ2 (alignment main effect + S\u00d7A interaction):** C2 vs C1 (13B alignment gain) and C4 vs C3 (70B alignment gain). The S\u00d7A interaction is tested by comparing the *difference* (C4\u2212C3) to (C2\u2212C1). If the alignment gain is smaller at 70B, size and alignment interact.\n- **Partial SQ3:** C3 vs C1 gives the raw size advantage before any alignment, establishing the \"pre-training-only\" size effect.\n\n**Cells NOT available in Tier 1:** Reduced-P variants, SFT-only (Partial-A) variants. These require Tier 2.\n\n### 2.3 Tier 2 \u2014 Extended Arm (Retraining / Proxy Checkpoints)\n\n**Purpose:** Answer SQ3 and the full S\u00d7P\u00d7A interaction. Requires a training budget.\n\n**Additional cells (6 new models):**\n\n| Cell ID | Model | S | P | A | How to obtain |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| C5 | 13B-50%P | 13 B | ~50 % tokens | None | Retrain from LLaMA-2-13B init, stop at 1.0 T tokens (of 2.0 T) |\n| C6 | 13B-25%P | 13 B | ~25 % tokens | None | Retrain, stop at 0.5 T tokens |\n| C7 | 70B-50%P | 70 B | ~50 % tokens | None | Retrain from LLaMA-2-70B init, stop at 1.0 T tokens |\n| C8 | 70B-25%P | 70 B | ~25 % tokens | None | Retrain, stop at 0.5 T tokens |\n| C9 | 13B-SFT-only | 13 B | Full | Partial (SFT, no RLHF) | Fine-tune LLaMA-2-13B-base with the SFT dataset only, skip RLHF |\n| C10 | 70B-SFT-only | 70 B | Full | Partial (SFT, no RLHF) | Fine-tune LLaMA-2-70B-base with the SFT dataset only, skip RLHF |\n\n**Fallback if retraining is infeasible:** Use proxy checkpoints from the literature (e.g., a 70B-class model trained on a known smaller corpus, or a community fine-tune that applied SFT without RLHF). The design should note that proxy checkpoints introduce an uncontrolled variable (different data mix, different hyperparameters) and results should be interpreted with that caveat.\n\n**Optional Tier 2 extension (if budget allows):** Cross the reduced-P and partial-A factors (e.g., 70B-50%P-SFT-only) to test the P\u00d7A interaction and the full three-way S\u00d7P\u00d7A interaction. This adds 4 more cells (C11\u2013C14) and roughly doubles Tier 2 training cost.\n\n### 2.4 Key Comparison Conditions (the cells that answer the PQ)\n\nThese are the contrasts the analysis will focus on, in priority order:\n\n| Priority | Contrast | Cells | Answers |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **K1** | Size at full alignment | C4 vs C2 | Baseline: does 70B beat 13B when both are well-trained and well-aligned? |\n| **K2** | Size at no alignment | C3 vs C1 | Raw pre-training size advantage (no confound from alignment) |\n| **K3** | Alignment gain at 13B | C2 vs C1 | How much does alignment help the smaller model? |\n| **K4** | Alignment gain at 70B | C4 vs C3 | How much does alignment help the larger model? |\n| **K5** | **Crossover (focal test)** | C7 or C8 vs C2 | Does a 70B with reduced pre-training lose to a 13B with full pre-training + full alignment? |\n| **K6** | **Crossover with partial alignment** | C10 vs C2 | Does a 70B with SFT-only alignment lose to a 13B with full alignment? |\n| **K7** | S\u00d7A interaction | (C4\u2212C3) vs (C2\u2212C1) | Is the alignment benefit size-dependent? |\n| **K8** | S\u00d7P interaction | (C4\u2212C2) vs (C7\u2212C5) [or C8\u2212C6] | Does the size advantage shrink as pre-training is reduced? |\n\nK5 and K6 are the **focal contrasts** \u2014 they directly test H\u2081. K1\u2013K4 are available in Tier 1; K5\u2013K8 require Tier 2.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Controls\n\nAll conditions must be evaluated under identical conditions to ensure that observed differences are attributable to the model, not the evaluation pipeline.\n\n| Control | Specification |\n|---|---|\n| **Prompt format** | Identical prompt template per benchmark. No system prompt, no few-shot examples unless specified by the benchmark protocol (e.g., MMLU 5-shot). The same template is applied to base and Chat models. |\n| **Decoding** | Greedy decoding (temperature = 0) for all multiple-choice and exact-match benchmarks. For generation-based benchmarks (IFEval), temperature = 0.7, top-p = 0.9, top-k = 50, max_new_tokens = 512. Fixed across all cells. |\n| **Random seed** | Seed = 42 for all stochastic operations (data shuffling, dropout during inference if applicable). |\n| **Evaluation harness** | `lm-evaluation-harness` (EleutherAI) or equivalent, pinned to a specific commit. Same harness version for all cells. |\n| **Tokenizer** | LLaMA-2 tokenizer (shared across 13B and 70B). Verify that the tokenizer file is identical across checkpoints. |\n| **Precision** | BF16 for all models. No mixed precision, no quantisation in the primary analysis. (INT4/AWQ is a supplementary sensitivity check only, see \u00a76.5.) |\n| **Hardware** | Same GPU tier for all models in a given run. If 70B requires 2 GPUs and 13B runs on 1, run 13B on the same 2-GPU node with the second GPU idle, to eliminate any node-level variability. |\n| **Batch size** | Batch size = 1 for generation-based benchmarks (IFEval). Batch size = 32 (or max that fits in memory) for multiple-choice benchmarks. |\n| **Data version** | Pin exact dataset versions (e.g., MMLU from `huggingface.co/datasets/cais/mmlu`, specific commit hash). Do not use \"latest.\" |\n| **No prompt engineering** | No system prompts, no \"You are a helpful assistant\" preambles, no chain-of-thought scaffolding. Raw model output only. |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Evaluation Battery\n\n### 4.1 Primary Benchmarks (3 \u2014 for the main analysis)\n\nSelected to **separate pre-training quality from alignment quality**:\n\n| Benchmark | Items | Format | Probes | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **MMLU** (5-shot) | 1,404 (all 57 subjects) or 1,000 (stratified subsample) | 4-way multiple choice | Pre-training: broad factual knowledge, reasoning | Size-sensitive. A larger model with full pre-training should dominate here. If a reduced-P 70B drops below a full-P 13B on MMLU, that is direct evidence that pre-training depth mediates the size advantage. |\n| **IFEval** (instruction-following) | 500 (validation split) | Generation, exact-match constraint verification | Alignment: ability to follow format/style constraints | Alignment-sensitive. A base model (no SFT) should score near 0; a Chat model should score substantially higher. This benchmark isolates the alignment pipeline from raw knowledge. |\n| **TruthfulQA** (multiple-choice) | 817 | 4-way multiple choice (truthful answer vs 3 plausible falsehoods) | Both: factual accuracy + calibrated honesty | Mixed sensitivity. Requires both knowledge (pre-training) and the ability to resist plausible-but-false answers (alignment). If a reduced-P 70B degrades here while a full-P 13B holds, it suggests pre-training quality matters even for \"honesty.\" |\n\n**Why these three:** MMLU is the cleanest pre-training probe (knowledge-intensive, minimal alignment dependence). IFEval is the cleanest alignment probe (constraint-following, minimal knowledge dependence). TruthfulQA sits in between and tests whether the two factors interact on a task that requires both. Together, they span the pre-training \u2194 alignment axis.\n\n### 4.2 Supplementary Benchmarks (secondary analysis, reported but not part of the primary decision rule)\n\n| Benchmark | Items | Probes |\n|---|---|---|\n| ARC-Challenge | 1,172 | Reasoning (pre-training) |\n| HellaSwag | 10,042 (val) | Common-sense completion (pre-training) |\n| WinoGrande | 440 (val) | Pronoun disambiguation (pre-training) |\n| ToxiGen | 2,770 | Safety / toxicity detection (alignment) |\n| MT-Bench (single-turn, 8 categories) | 80 | Instruction following, LLM-judged (alignment) |\n| Perplexity on C4 / PG19 held-out | 500 documents | Pre-training convergence (diagnostic) |\n\nThese are reported in supplementary tables and figures but do not enter the pre-registered decision rule (\u00a76.3).\n\n### 4.3 Scoring\n\n- **MMLU, TruthfulQA, ARC, HellaSwag, WinoGrande:** Accuracy (fraction correct). For MMLU, report both overall and per-subject-group (STEM, humanities, social sciences, other).\n- **IFEval:** Strict accuracy (fraction of prompts where all constraints are satisfied). Also report \"relaxed\" accuracy (fraction where \u2265 80 % of constraints are met) as a secondary metric.\n- **ToxiGen:** F1 on toxic/non-toxic classification.\n- **MT-Bench:** Mean LLM-judge score (1\u201310 scale), using a fixed judge model (e.g., GPT-4 or LLaMA-2-70B-Chat as judge, specified in advance).\n- **Perplexity:** Geometric mean perplexity on held-out documents.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Analysis Plan\n\n### 5.1 Descriptive Analysis\n\n- Mean accuracy/score per cell per benchmark, with 95 % bootstrap CIs (10,000 resamples, resampling items within benchmark).\n- Heatmap: rows = cells (C1\u2013C10 or C1\u2013C14), columns = benchmarks, colour = score. Annotate with the S, P, A factor levels.\n- Grouped bar charts for each K-contrast (\u00a72.4), with CI error bars.\n\n### 5.2 Inferential Analysis\n\n**Model:** Mixed-effects regression for each benchmark:\n\n```\nScore_ij = \u03b2\u2080 + \u03b2\u2081\u00b7S_i + \u03b2\u2082\u00b7P_i + \u03b2\u2083\u00b7A_i\n         + \u03b2\u2084\u00b7(S\u00d7P)_i + \u03b2\u2085\u00b7(S\u00d7A)_i + \u03b2\u2086\u00b7(P\u00d7A)_i + \u03b2\u2087\u00b7(S\u00d7P\u00d7A)_i\n         + u_j + \u03b5_ij\n```\n\nwhere `i` indexes the model cell, `j` indexes the evaluation item, `u_j ~ N(0, \u03c3\u00b2_u)` is a random intercept for item (to account for item difficulty), and `\u03b5_ij ~ N(0, \u03c3\u00b2_\u03b5)` is residual. S, P, A are coded as ordinal numeric variables (13B=0, 70B=1; Full=2, Reduced=1, Severely Reduced=0; Full=2, Partial=1, None=0).\n\n- **Tier 1 (4 cells):** Fit the model with S and A as fixed factors, S\u00d7A interaction. P is not varied (all Full), so P terms are dropped. This is a 2\u00d72 ANOVA with item-level random effects.\n- **Tier 2 (10\u201314 cells):** Fit the full model with all three factors and interactions.\n\n**Post-hoc:** For significant interactions, simple-effects analysis at each level of the other factor. Report partial \u03b7\u00b2 for effect sizes.\n\n### 5.3 Pre-Registered Decision Rule\n\nThis is the critical section. Without a pre-specified threshold, the crossover analysis is descriptive only. The following rules are **fixed before any results are inspected**:\n\n**Definitions:**\n- Let \u0394_full = Score(70B, Full-P, Full-A) \u2212 Score(13B, Full-P, Full-A) [i.e., K1]. This is the \"full size advantage.\"\n- Let \u0394_test = Score(70B, P*, A*) \u2212 Score(13B, Full-P, Full-A) [i.e., K5 or K6]. This is the \"challenged size advantage.\"\n- Let \u03c1 = \u0394_test / \u0394_full (the ratio of the challenged advantage to the full advantage).\n\n**Decision categories (per benchmark, per focal contrast):**\n\n| Category | Condition | Interpretation |\n|---|---|---|\n| **NEGATION** | \u0394_test < 0 AND the upper bound of the 95 % bootstrap CI for \u0394_test is < 0 | The larger model with reduced P or A *loses* to the smaller, fully-trained, fully-aligned model. Size advantage is negated. |\n| **SIGNIFICANT REDUCTION** | \u0394_test > 0, but \u03c1 < 0.5 AND the 95 % CI for (\u03c1 \u2212 1) does not include 0 (i.e., the reduction from full is statistically significant) AND the 95 % CI for \u0394_test does not include 0 | The size advantage is halved or more. Alignment or pre-training deficiency materially erodes the benefit of extra parameters. |\n| **NO SIGNIFICANT EFFECT** | \u0394_test > 0, \u03c1 \u2265 0.5, or CIs include 0 | The size advantage is preserved (perhaps slightly reduced but not significantly so). |\n\n**Interaction significance threshold:**\n- S\u00d7A interaction (Tier 1): p < 0.01 (Bonferroni-corrected for 3 primary benchmarks).\n- S\u00d7P interaction (Tier 2): p < 0.01 (Bonferroni-corrected for 3 primary benchmarks).\n- S\u00d7P\u00d7A three-way (Tier 2 extension): p < 0.005.\n\n**Effect-size threshold:**\n- For the focal contrasts (K5, K6): report Cohen's d (standardised mean difference, using the pooled SD of the 13B-Full-P-Full-A group as the denominator).\n- d \u2265 0.8 (large) required to claim NEGATION is practically meaningful, not just statistically significant.\n- 0.2 \u2264 d < 0.8 for SIGNIFICANT REDUCTION.\n\n**Multiple-comparison control:**\n- Primary analysis: 3 benchmarks \u00d7 2 focal contrasts (K5, K6) = 6 tests. Bonferroni: \u03b1 = 0.05/6 \u2248 0.0083 per test for the NEGATION claim.\n- For SIGNIFICANT REDUCTION: \u03b1 = 0.05/6 per test.\n- Secondary contrasts (K1\u2013K4, K7, K8): \u03b1 = 0.05, no correction (descriptive).\n\n**Pre-registration:** The decision rule, benchmarks, item counts, and coding scheme must be written down and timestamped (e.g., uploaded to OSF or a version-controlled repo) **before** the first model evaluation is run. No post-hoc revision.\n\n### 5.4 Crossover Analysis\n\n- For each P level (Full, Reduced, Severely Reduced), plot Score vs. S (13B, 70B) for each A level. Three lines per panel (one per A level), x-axis = parameter count (log scale), y-axis = score.\n- Identify the **crossover point**: the P level (interpolated) at which the 70B line crosses below the 13B line, for each A level.\n- Report the crossover P level as a percentage of full pre-training tokens (e.g., \"at 40 % of full pre-training, the 70B model's MMLU score falls below the 13B-Chat score\").\n- If no crossover occurs within the tested P range, report the P level at which the size advantage is smallest (the \"nadir\").\n\n### 5.5 Sensitivity Analyses\n\n1. **Quantisation:** Repeat K1 and K5 with INT4 (AWQ) weights. If the size advantage changes by > 2 percentage points, flag quantisation as a potential confound.\n2. **Decoding temperature:** Repeat IFEval at temperature = 0 (greedy) and temperature = 0.7. Report both.\n3. **Subsample stability:** For MMLU, compute scores on 5 independent random subsamples of 500 items. Report the standard deviation across subsamples. If SD > 1 percentage point, flag item-sampling variance as a concern.\n4. **Prompt sensitivity (IFEval only):** Evaluate 3 paraphrased versions of 100 IFEval prompts. Report the variance in constraint-satisfaction rate.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Feasibility\n\n### 6.1 Tier 1 \u2014 Feasible Core\n\n| Item | Specification |\n|---|---|\n| **Models** | 4 public checkpoints: Llama-2-13b-hf, Llama-2-13b-chat-hf, Llama-2-70b-hf, Llama-2-70b-chat-hf |\n| **Total weight download** | 13b: ~26 GB each \u00d7 2 = 52 GB; 70b: ~140 GB each \u00d7 2 = 280 GB; **Total: ~332 GB** |\n| **Minimum hardware** | **2 \u00d7 NVIDIA A100-80 GB** (or 1 \u00d7 H100-80 GB + 1 \u00d7 A100-80 GB). 70B in BF16 requires ~140 GB VRAM \u2192 2 \u00d7 80 GB GPUs. 13B in BF16 requires ~26 GB \u2192 fits on 1 GPU. Run 13B models on the same 2-GPU node (second GPU idle) for hardware parity. |\n| **Alternative** | 1 \u00d7 A100-80 GB with INT4 quantisation (AWQ): 70B \u2248 35 GB, 13B \u2248 7 GB. Single-GPU feasible but quantisation is a sensitivity check, not primary. |\n| **CPU/RAM** | 64+ cores, 256 GB system RAM (for data loading, tokenisation). |\n| **Disk** | 500 GB free (weights + evaluation datasets + logs). |\n| **Software** | Python 3.10+, `torch` \u2265 2.1, `transformers` \u2265 4.31, `accelerate`, `lm-evaluation-harness` (pinned commit), `datasets`. |\n| **Inference time estimate** | Per 70B model: MMLU (1000 items \u00d7 ~0.5 s) + IFEval (500 items \u00d7 ~3 s) + TruthfulQA (817 items \u00d7 ~0.5 s) \u2248 500 + 1500 + 400 = **~2,400 s \u2248 40 min**. Per 13B model: ~4\u00d7 faster \u2248 **~10 min**. 4 models total: **~1.5 hours** pure inference. |\n| **Wall-clock** | **1 day** (including environment setup, data download, sanity checks, running all 4 models, collecting results, initial analysis). |\n| **Cost tier** | **Low.** Cloud: 2 \u00d7 A100-80 GB \u2248 $30\u201340/hr \u2192 ~$30\u201340 total for 1 day. Internal cluster: negligible. |\n\n### 6.2 Tier 2 \u2014 Extended Arm\n\n| Item | Specification |\n|---|---|\n| **Models** | 6 additional checkpoints (C5\u2013C10), optionally 4 more (C11\u2013C14) |\n| **Training (reduced-P cells C5\u2013C8)** | Retrain from LLaMA-2 init with reduced token budget. 70B at 50 % tokens (1.0 T): ~100\u2013200 A100-days (depending on batch size, sequence length, and hardware efficiency). 13B at 50 %: ~15\u201330 A100-days. 25 % variants: half of those. **Total training: ~200\u2013500 A100-days** for all 4 reduced-P models. |\n| **Fine-tuning (partial-A cells C9\u2013C10)** | SFT on LLaMA-2-Chat SFT dataset (publicly released, ~117K examples). 70B SFT: ~5\u201310 A100-days. 13B SFT: ~1\u20132 A100-days. **Total: ~10 A100-days.** |\n| **Minimum training hardware** | **8 \u00d7 A100-80 GB** (1 node) for 70B training. 4 \u00d7 A100-80 GB sufficient for 13B. |\n| **Inference (Tier 2 cells)** | Same as Tier 1 per model. 6 additional models \u00d7 ~10\u201340 min = **~3\u20134 hours** inference. |\n| **Wall-clock** | **2\u20136 weeks**, dominated by 70B retraining. If proxy checkpoints are used instead of retraining, wall-clock drops to **~2 days** (inference only). |\n| **Cost tier** | **High.** 8 \u00d7 A100-80 GB for 200\u2013500 GPU-days \u2248 $50,000\u2013$150,000+ (cloud) or equivalent internal cluster allocation. SFT-only (C9, C10) is much cheaper: ~$1,000\u2013$3,000. |\n| **Fallback** | If full retraining is infeasible, run only C9 and C10 (SFT-only, ~10 A100-days) and use proxy checkpoints for the reduced-P cells. This reduces Tier 2 to a **medium cost tier** (~$5,000\u2013$15,000) and ~1\u20132 weeks. |\n\n### 6.3 Software Requirements (Both Tiers)\n\n```\npython >= 3.10\ntorch >= 2.1 (CUDA 12.1+)\ntransformers >= 4.31\naccelerate >= 0.25\ndatasets >= 2.14\nlm-evaluation-harness (pinned commit)\nnumpy, scipy, pandas, matplotlib, seaborn\nstatsmodels or pingouin (for ANOVA / mixed-effects)\n```\n\nNo GPU-specific software beyond CUDA/cuDNN. No distributed training framework required for Tier 1 (inference only). Tier 2 training requires `deepspeed` or `FSDP` for 70B.\n\n### 6.4 Summary: What You Need to Run Each Tier\n\n| | Tier 1 (Feasible Core) | Tier 2 (Extended Arm) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Answers** | SQ1, SQ2, partial SQ3 | SQ3, full S\u00d7P\u00d7A, focal H\u2081 test |\n| **GPU** | 2 \u00d7 A100-80 GB | 8 \u00d7 A100-80 GB (training) + 2 \u00d7 A100-80 GB (inference) |\n| **Wall-clock** | ~1 day | 2\u20136 weeks (retraining) or ~2 days (proxy) |\n| **Cost** | ~$30\u201340 (cloud) | $50K\u2013$150K (retraining) or $5K\u2013$15K (proxy) |\n| **Data needed** | 4 public checkpoints + 3 benchmark datasets (~2 GB) | + LLaMA-2 pre-training data (2.0 T tokens) or proxy checkpoints + SFT dataset |\n| **Feasibility** | Any lab with 2 \u00d7 A100 | Serious training budget or access to proxy checkpoints |\n\n---\n\n## 7. Limitations\n\n1. **LLaMA-2-base is a proxy for \"no alignment,\" not a true unaligned control.** The base model underwent the full 2.0 T-token pre-training recipe, including the same data mix, learning rate schedule, and optimiser as the Chat models. It was simply not instruction-tuned or preference-optimised. Therefore, the \"None\" level of factor A does not mean \"the model was trained without any alignment-adjacent objective\"; it means \"the model was not post-trained with SFT/RLHF.\" The alignment main effect (A) should be interpreted as the effect of *post-training alignment* (SFT + RLHF), not the effect of *all alignment-related training decisions*. A truly unaligned control would require retraining the base model with a modified pre-training objective that explicitly avoids alignment-relevant signals (e.g., excluding safety-relevant data from the pre-training mix), which is not feasible within this design. **Readers should not interpret the A main effect as the total contribution of \"alignment\" in a broad sense; it is specifically the contribution of the SFT + RLHF post-training pipeline.**\n\n2. **Reduced-P cells require retraining or proxies.** The \"insufficient pre-training\" condition is operationalised as a token-budget reduction (50 %, 25 %). This is a simplification: in practice, \"insufficient\" could also mean a worse data mix, a suboptimal learning rate schedule, or an under-converged optimiser state. The token-budget reduction isolates one specific axis of pre-training insufficiency (quantity) but not others (quality, diversity). Results should be generalised to \"reduced pre-training data volume\" specifically, not to all forms of pre-training insufficiency.\n\n3. **LLaMA-2-specific.** All findings are specific to the LLaMA-2 architecture, training recipe, and data mix. The size range (13B\u201370B) and the specific alignment pipeline (SFT + RLHF) may not generalise to other model families (e.g., LLaMA-3, Mistral, Gemma) or to different size ranges (e.g., 7B vs 13B, where the size gap is smaller).\n\n4. **Benchmarks are static.** MMLU, IFEval, and TruthfulQA are fixed benchmarks. They do not capture emergent capabilities (e.g., long-context reasoning, tool use) that might scale differently with size vs. alignment.\n\n5. **Single-seed evaluation (primary).** The primary analysis uses greedy decoding (temperature = 0), which is deterministic. This eliminates sampling variance but means the results reflect a single point estimate per item. The sensitivity analysis (\u00a75.5, item 3) partially addresses this with subsampling.\n\n6. **No human evaluation.** MT-Bench (supplementary) uses an LLM judge. No human raters are involved. For IFEval, the constraint verification is automated and deterministic, which is a strength.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Statement\n\n**This document is an experiment design. No experiment has been run, no model has been evaluated, and no results are reported.** The environment in which this design was developed (`/workspace`, bubblewrap sandbox, CPU-only, no network, no GPU, no ML libraries, no model weights) does not support execution of any part of this design. The design is a blueprint for execution in a properly provisioned environment with GPU access, network connectivity for weight downloads, and the software stack specified in \u00a76.3.\n\n---\n\n## Appendix A: Cell-to-Model Mapping (Quick Reference)\n\n| Cell | S | P | A | Model | Tier |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| C1 | 13 B | Full | None | Llama-2-13b-hf | 1 |\n| C2 | 13 B | Full | Full | Llama-2-13b-chat-hf | 1 |\n| C3 | 70 B | Full | None | Llama-2-70b-hf | 1 |\n| C4 | 70 B | Full | Full | Llama-2-70b-chat-hf | 1 |\n| C5 | 13 B | 50 % | None | Retrain / proxy | 2 |\n| C6 | 13 B | 25 % | None | Retrain / proxy | 2 |\n| C7 | 70 B | 50 % | None | Retrain / proxy | 2 |\n| C8 | 70 B | 25 % | None | Retrain / proxy | 2 |\n| C9 | 13 B | Full | Partial | SFT-only fine-tune | 2 |\n| C10 | 70 B | Full | Partial | SFT-only fine-tune | 2 |\n| C11\u2013C14 | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 | Optional P\u00d7A crosses | 2-ext |\n\n## Appendix B: Pre-Registered Decision Rule (Summary Card)\n\n```\nFOR EACH primary benchmark (MMLU, IFEval, TruthfulQA)\nAND EACH focal contrast (K5: 70B-ReducedP vs 13B-Full, K6: 70B-PartialA vs 13B-Full):\n\n  Compute \u0394_test = Score(70B variant) \u2212 Score(13B-Full-P-Full-A)\n  Compute 95% bootstrap CI for \u0394_test (10,000 resamples)\n  Compute \u03c1 = \u0394_test / \u0394_full  where \u0394_full = Score(70B-Full) \u2212 Score(13B-Full)\n\n  IF \u0394_test < 0 AND CI_upper(\u0394_test) < 0:\n      \u2192 NEGATION (size advantage eliminated)\n      \u2192 Require Cohen's d \u2265 0.8 for practical significance\n  ELSE IF \u0394_test > 0 AND \u03c1 < 0.5 AND CI(\u03c1\u22121) excludes 0:\n      \u2192 SIGNIFICANT REDUCTION (size advantage halved)\n      \u2192 Require 0.2 \u2264 |d| < 0.8\n  ELSE:\n      \u2192 NO SIGNIFICANT EFFECT\n\n  Bonferroni \u03b1 = 0.0083 per test (6 tests total)\n  Interaction p < 0.01 (Bonferroni for 3 benchmarks)\n```", "stage": "result", "evidence": "Workspace inspection: /workspace contains only task.json (520 bytes) with the question, context, and \"Design-only task\" note. No datasets, scripts, weights, or docs. Environment: Python 3.14.4, 224 CPU cores, ~2 TB RAM, no GPU (nvidia-smi fails), no network (ping/urllib blocked), no torch/transformers/vllm/llama.cpp, no HF cache, no model weights anywhere (find / for .safetensors returns empty), 1006 GB tmpfs. task.json explicitly states \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\" All design decisions (factorial structure, two-tier architecture, benchmark selection, decision rule, feasibility estimates) are grounded in the publicly documented LLaMA-2 model family (Touvron et al., 2023: 13B and 70B variants, base and Chat checkpoints, 2.0T-token pre-training, SFT+RLHF alignment) and standard ML evaluation practice (lm-evaluation-harness, MMLU, IFEval, TruthfulQA). Feasibility estimates (VRAM require